// EXPECT: E0285 — a `#simd` width no machine register has (ADR-0148 §2)
//
// `[4]s64` is thirty-two bytes, and a vector operation compiles at exactly sixteen: `iadd` on an
// `I64X4` fails Cranelift's backend with `Unexpected SSA-value type`, which is what probing found
// before this feature was designed. The type *constructor* is happy to make one, so a plan built on
// it would have looked fine right up to the first compile.
//
// **This is a machine fact in the language, deliberately.** A vector type exists because the machine
// has vector registers; a portable-looking `#simd [8]s64` would have to be split into four of them or
// scalarised into a loop, and ADR-0058 §3's rule — a directive silently ignored is worse than one
// rejected — applies with unusual force to a construct a reader chose for speed. Letting LLVM split
// while Cranelift could not would also make the differential harness compare two different programs.
//
// **One** refusal here, per this directory's contract. The other shapes E0285 covers:
//
//   * an element that is not a numeric scalar — `#simd [2]string`, a pointer, or a `bool`, none of
//     which a lane can hold arithmetic for;
//   * `/` and `%` on an integer vector, because no machine has an integer vector divide — refused
//     rather than scalarised, since a silent pessimisation in a construct chosen for speed is the
//     performance-domain twin of a silent miscompile (§3);
//   * `+ - *` on an integer vector, which would have to trap and cannot: no target has a per-lane
//     overflow flag, so the wrapping spellings `+% -% *%` are what an integer vector has (§6).
//
// The message names the six legal shapes rather than stating the rule, because the rule is the
// *reason* and the shapes are the answer: a reader who wrote `[4]s64` wants to be told `[2]s64`
// exists.
main :: () {
    wide: #simd [4]s64;
}
